<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="6" to="9" step="1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="b" from="2" to="4" step="1" exclude="2*$a"/>

  <function name="f">x^$b/(1+x^$a)</function>

</setup>

<p>
  Our goal in this exercise is to find the general antiderivative of the function
  <me>
    f(x) = \frac{x^$b}{1+x^$a}
  </me>
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        Use a familiar Taylor series expansion along with algebraic techniques to find the first four nonzero terms of the Taylor series centered at <m>0</m> for <m>f(x)</m>.  State those four respective terms in the provided blanks below.
      </p>
      <p sectionWideCheckWork forceIndividualAnswerColoring>
        <m>f(x) = </m><answer>x^$b</answer><m>+</m><answer>-x^($a+$b)</answer><m>+</m><answer>x^(2*$a+$b)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-x^(3*$a+$b)</answer><m>+ \cdots \hspace{.1in}</m>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Determine the open interval of convergence for the Taylor series of <m>f(x) = \frac{x^$b}{1+x^$a}</m>.  If the series converges for all real numbers, enter <m>(-\text{infinity}, \text{infinity})</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Taylor series of <m>f(x)</m> converges on <answer><interval>(-1,1)</interval></answer>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Use the Power Series Differentiation and Integration Theorem to find the first <em>five</em> nonzero terms of the Taylor series for <m>F(x)</m>, the general antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m>.  Enter those terms in order of increasing degree in the blanks provided; if you need to include a constant of integration, use <q><m>C</m></q>.
      </p>
      <p sectionWideCheckWork forceIndividualAnswerColoring>
        <m>F(x) = </m><answer name="constant">C</answer><m>+</m><answer>1/($b+1)*x^($b+1)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-1/($a+$b+1)*x^($a+$b+1)</answer><m>+</m><answer>1/(2*$a+$b+1)*x^(2*$a+$b+1)</answer><m>+</m><answer>-1/(3*$a+$b+1)*x^(3*$a+$b+1)</answer><m>+ \cdots \hspace{.1in}</m>
      </p>
      <feedback condition="$constant = 1/($b+1)*x^($b+1)">The constant of integration needs to come first, and the <math simplify>1/($b+1)*x^($b+1)</math> term will be second.</feedback>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Determine the open interval of convergence for the Taylor series of <m>F(x)</m>.  If the series converges for all real numbers, enter <m>(-\text{infinity}, \text{infinity})</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Taylor series of <m>F(x)</m> converges on <answer><interval>(-1,1)</interval></answer>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>